# A floor and a lamp, and nothing else: the witness for bias.
#
#   --integrator naive|path --renderer mt --threads 8 --seed 0
#   --samples_ppx 1024 --max_depth 4 --output_width 256 --output_height 192
#
# WHAT IT ISOLATES
#
# Whether `naive` and `path` agree. They estimate the same integral by routes that share nothing —
# `naive` reaches the lamp only by sampling the bsdf and never consults `Scene::lights`, `path`
# aims at it through next event estimation — so a factor lost anywhere in the area-light chain
# separates them. A missing cosine, an inverse square applied twice, a measure left unconverted:
# each scales the image without changing its shape, which the eye does not catch and the other
# estimator does.
#
# WHY IT IS BUILT THIS WAY
#
# The point is that `naive` converge *quickly*, and everything here serves that. The panel is large
# and close, so it subtends a great deal of the hemisphere: seen from the centre of the floor its
# form factor is 0.36, meaning better than one cosine-weighted ray in three lands on it. There are
# no walls and no declared `light`, so almost all of the light is direct and a short path is enough
# — `--max_depth 4` already has the answer.
#
# Compare with `cornell_box_exact.stage`, which is the opposite on both counts: a small panel in a
# closed white room, where `naive` needed 16384 paths per pixel. That scene says whether the
# absolute level is right against measured data; this one is the cheap loop to run while working.
#
# FRAMING
#
# The camera hangs just under the panel and looks straight down, so the lamp is *behind* it and
# cannot enter the frame however wide the field of view is opened. Framing it out by aiming away
# would be a tuning that the next change to the scene breaks; putting it behind the lens is a
# property of the arrangement.
#
# That matters to the measurement and is not cosmetic: a source in frame saturates its pixels, and
# a mean then speaks partly for light the encoding has already cut off — `image_stats` prints the
# saturated count precisely so that this cannot pass unnoticed.
#
# `up` points along −z because the view direction is −y: a camera looking straight down cannot be
# told that up is +y, the two being parallel and their cross product zero.

camera pin_hole
  pos 0.0 2.9 0.0
  look 0.0 0.0 0.0
  up 0.0 0.0 -1.0

scene

  # The floor, at y = 0. A `rectangle` is authored there already, so it needs no placement at all —
  # and an identity `transform` is not free, `Transformed` renormalising the ray it passes down.
  object simple
    rectangle 12 12
    lambertian color 0.6 0.6 0.6

  # The lamp, 4 by 4, three units up. Emission is one-sided along the normal, and a `rectangle`
  # faces +Y, so the half turn about x is what aims it at the floor rather than at the sky.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 4 4
      diffuse_light color 1.5 1.5 1.5
    transform {
      rotate_x 3.14159265
      translate 0.0 3.0 0.0
    }
